{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh6gLyx6nALv9aaK1yEm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I get **Dq ≈ 983 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 883 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 3 190 cm⁻¹**. I fitted Dq and B to the three spin-allowed bands, then used the spin-forbidden ¹E band to fix C.\n\n**1. Spin-allowed bands give Dq and B.** For octahedral d⁸ (ground term ³A₂g):\n- ν₁(³T₂) = 10Dq\n- ν₂, ν₃(³T₁, ³T₁(2)) = 7.5B + 15Dq ∓ ½√(225B² − 180B·Dq + 100Dq²)\n\nThese three energies do not depend on C. Solving exactly would give Dq = 977 cm⁻¹ from ν₁ and B ≈ 899 cm⁻¹ from ν₂ + ν₃. But then the predicted ν₃ − ν₂ is about 10 910 cm⁻¹, against 10 690 cm⁻¹ observed. So I did a least-squares fit to all three bands instead:\n- Dq = 983 cm⁻¹\n- B = 883 cm⁻¹\n- Calculated bands: 9 835, 15 992 and 26 751 cm⁻¹\n- Root-mean-square error: about 50 cm⁻¹\n\n**2. The ¹E band gives C.** The energy of ¹E(a) depends strongly on C, rising by about 2 cm⁻¹ for every 1 cm⁻¹ increase in C. I diagonalised the full d⁸ matrices with Dq = 983 and B = 883:\n- With C = 4 850 cm⁻¹ (the tool's default), ¹E sits at 16 316 cm⁻¹.\n- With C = 3 200 cm⁻¹, ¹E sits at 13 016 cm⁻¹.\n\nInterpolating to the observed 12 987 cm⁻¹ gives C ≈ 3 186 cm⁻¹, so C/B ≈ 3.6. I did not re-diagonalise at exactly 3 186 cm⁻¹, but the step from 3 200 is small, so the error should be minor.\n\nB ≈ 883 cm⁻¹ is about 85% of the free-ion Ni²⁺ value (about 1 040 cm⁻¹), so the Ni–O bonds are only moderately covalent.\n\n{\"Dq\": 983, \"B\": 883, \"C\": 3186, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq and B fitted to the three spin-allowed triplet bands; C fixed by matching the 1E band at 12987 cm-1.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1373,"cache_read_input_tokens":10267,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1373,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_